n) and J3 (White). A pung of J2 would give 2 points (Dragon Pung). A pung of J3 would also give 2 points. But since they have both, maybe discarding one of them is good. However, the user's hand has J2 and J3. If they discard J3, they can still get another J3. But the Dragon Pung is worth 2 points each. So maybe similar value.\n\nBut the F3 (West Wind) is the prevailing wind, so a pung there would give 2 points for Prevalent Wind. That's a specific pattern. So that might be better.\n\nSo between discarding F3 and J3, F3 might be better.\n\nLooking at other options: discarding T6 or B7. If they discard T6, they have one left. If they draw another T6, they can form a pung. That's a pair in a suit, which could lead to a pung. But pung in a suit is worth 2 points (Pung of Terminals or Honors if it's a terminal, but T6 is not a terminal. Terminals are 1 and 9. So T6 is a middle tile. So a pung of T6 would be worth 2 points under Pung of Terminals or Honors? No, because it's not a terminal or honor. Wait, Pung of Terminals or Honors is for pungs of terminal tiles (1 or 9) or honor tiles. T6 is a middle tile, so a pung of T6 would not count under that. So it would just be a regular pung, which is part of All Pungs (4 points) if they have four pungs. But in this case, if they form a pung of T6, it's just one pung. So not as valuable as a pung in honors.\n\nTherefore, discarding F3 or J3 for potential honor pungs is better than discarding T6 or B7.\n\nSo between F3 and J3. F3 is West Wind, which is the prevailing wind. A pung of F3 would give 2 points for Prevalent Wind. J3 is White Dragon, a pung would give 2 points for Dragon Pung. Both are 2 points. So similar value. Which one is better?\n\nThe user's hand already has F3 and F4. If they discard F3, they can still have F4. If they get another F3, they can form a pung. If they discard J3, they can still have J2. So both are possible.\n\nBut since F3 is the prevailing wind, maybe it's better to focus on that. Also, the user 