s a pair. If I play B7, I lose the pair. That's not good. \n\nSo the options are Play W2, B4, F4, W1, W8, or B7. \n\nIf I play W2, I can still keep W1 as a pair and try to form a W1-W2-W3 Chow. But I need a W3. If I play W2, I might be giving it to someone else. \n\nIf I play B4, I can try to form a B4-B5-B6 Chow. But again, need B5 and B6. \n\nF4 is an honor tile. If I play F4, maybe another player can Peng it. But since I'm the one discarding, it's better to keep it. \n\nW1 is part of a pair. If I play W1, I lose the pair. \n\nW8 is part of the existing meld. Playing it might not help. \n\nB7 is a pair. Playing it would be bad. \n\nSo between W2, B4, and F4. \n\nI think playing W2 might be better because if I can get a W3, I can form a Chow. But I need to consider the game state. The next player has a Peng of J1 and has played J2, J3, etc. The player across has a concealed Kong. The previous player has played various tiles. \n\nAlternatively, if I play B4, maybe I can form a B4-B5-B6 Chow if I get those tiles. But the unshown B5 has 4 left, which is available. \n\nBut in my hand, I have B4. If I play B4, I can still keep B7 as a pair. \n\nAnother angle: my current hand has two pairs (W1 and B7). If I can form two more melds, that's four melds and a pair. Let's see. \n\nIf I have W1 (2), maybe I can form a W1-W2-W3 Chow. If I have W1 and W2, but need W3. \n\nIf I have B4, maybe form a B4-B5-B6 Chow. \n\nBut I don't have B5 or B6. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a B7-B8-B9 Chow. I have B7, need B8 and B9. \n\nSo the best option is to play a tile that allows me to form one of these Chows. \n\nIf I play W2, I can keep W1 and try for W3. \n\nIf I play B4, I can try for B5 and B6. \n\nWhich is more likely? Well, W3 has 3 left, B5 has 4 left. \n\nBut I have W1 and W2, which are consecutive. If I play W2, I lose the chance to form that Chow. Wait, no. If I have W1 (2) and W2 (1), and play W2, I still have W1 (2). But to form a W1-W2-W3 Chow, I need to have W1, W2, and